questions.question:
What is the output of the following program segment?
```c
#include <stdio.h>
void main()
{ int x=4, y=3, z;
  z = x---y;
  printf("%d,%d,%d", x, y, z);
}
```

questions.answer:
**a)** A header file declares interfaces such as function prototypes, macros, and types; it is included with `#include <stdio.h>` or `#include "my.h"`. C comments are `/* block comment */` and, in modern C, `// line comment`.

**b)** Bitwise operators act on bits: `&`, `|`, `^`, `~`, `<<`, `>>`. Example: `6 & 3` is 2. `break` exits the nearest loop or switch; `goto label;` transfers control to a labelled statement. Example:
```c
for (int i=0; i<10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) break;
}
if (error) goto cleanup;
cleanup: free(p);
```

**a)** A nested `if-else` places one conditional inside another:
```c
if (marks >= 40) {
    if (marks >= 75) printf("Distinction");
    else printf("Pass");
} else printf("Fail");
```

**b)** `while` checks its condition before each iteration and may execute zero times. `do while` checks after the body, so the body executes at least once.

**c)** ```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int a, b, t;
    scanf("%d%d", &a, &b);
    while (b) { t = a % b; a = b; b = t; }
    printf("GCD = %d\n", a);
    return 0;
}
```

**a)** `switch` selects one branch by matching an integral expression against `case` labels; `break` prevents fall-through and `default` handles no match:
```c
switch (choice) {
case 1: printf("one"); break;
case 2: printf("two"); break;
default: printf("other");
}
```

**b)** ```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int n, i, term = 0, s = 0;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) { term += i; s += term; }
    printf("%d\n", s);
    return 0;
}
```

**a)** `strcpy(dest, src)` copies `src` including its null terminator into `dest`; `strcat(dest, src)` appends `src` to the end of `dest`. The destination must have enough space.

**b)** ```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(void) {
    char s[100]; int i = 0, j;
    scanf("%99s", s);
    j = (int)strlen(s) - 1;
    while (i < j) if (s[i++] != s[j--]) { printf("Not Palindrome\n"); return 0; }
    printf("Palindrome\n");
    return 0;
}
```

**a)** A function is a named reusable block that takes parameters and optionally returns a value. Library functions such as `printf()` and `strlen()` are supplied by the standard library; user-defined functions are written by the programmer.

**b)** ```c
#include <stdio.h>
long fact(int n) { return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * fact(n - 1); }
int main(void) { int n; scanf("%d", &n); printf("%ld\n", fact(n)); return 0; }
```

**a)** An array is a fixed-size contiguous collection of elements of one type. A one-dimensional array is declared as `int a[10];`.

**b)** ```c
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    int a[3][3], i, j;
    for (i=0;i<3;i++) for (j=0;j<3;j++) scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);
    for (i=0;i<3;i++) { for (j=0;j<3;j++) printf("%d ", a[i][j]); printf("\n"); }
    for (i=0;i<3;i++) { for (j=0;j<3;j++) printf("%d ", a[j][i]); printf("\n"); }
    return 0;
}
```

**a)** The conditional operator has the form `condition ? expr1 : expr2`. Example: `max = (a > b) ? a : b;`.

**b)** An actual argument is the value/expression supplied at the call site; a formal parameter is the variable declared in the function definition that receives that value.

